⟦P0⟧Accessing relational databases⟦P1⟧Using Go, you can incorporate a wide variety of databases and data access⟦P2⟧approaches into your applications. Topics in this section describe how to use⟦P3⟧the standard library's ⟦P4⟧⟦P5⟧⟦PB1_6⟧⟦P7⟧package to access relational databases.⟦P8⟧For an introductory tutorial to data access with Go, please see⟦P9⟧⟦P10⟧Tutorial: Accessing a relational database⟦PB1_11⟧.⟦P12⟧Go supports other data access technologies as well, including ⟦P13⟧ libraries⟦P14⟧for higher-level access to relational databases, and also non-relational⟦P15⟧NoSQL data stores.⟦P16⟧⟦P17⟧Object-relational mapping (⟦P18⟧) libraries.⟦P19⟧ While the ⟦P20⟧⟦P21⟧package includes functions for lower-level data access logic, you can⟦P22⟧also use Go to access data stores at a higher abstraction level. For more⟦P23⟧about two popular object-relational mapping (⟦P24⟧) libraries for Go, see⟦P25⟧⟦P26⟧GORM⟦PB1_27⟧ (⟦P28⟧package reference⟦PB1_29⟧)⟦P30⟧and ⟦P31⟧ent⟦PB1_32⟧ (⟦P33⟧package reference⟦PB1_34⟧).⟦P35⟧⟦P36⟧NoSQL data stores.⟦P37⟧ The Go community has developed drivers for the⟦P38⟧majority of NoSQL data stores, including ⟦P39⟧MongoDB⟦PB1_40⟧⟦P41⟧and ⟦P42⟧Couchbase⟦PB1_43⟧.⟦P44⟧You can search ⟦P45⟧⟦P46⟧⟦PB1_47⟧ for more.⟦P48⟧Supported database management systems ⟦P49⟧⟦P50⟧Go supports all of the most common relational database management systems,⟦P51⟧including MySQL, Oracle, Postgres, ⟦P52⟧ Server, SQLite, and more.⟦P53⟧You'll find a complete list of drivers at the⟦P54⟧⟦P55⟧SQLDrivers⟦PB1_56⟧ page.⟦P57⟧Functions to execute queries or make database changes ⟦P58⟧⟦P59⟧The ⟦P60⟧ package includes functions specifically designed for the⟦P61⟧kind of database operation you're executing. For example, while you can use⟦P62⟧⟦P63⟧ or ⟦P64⟧ to execute queries, ⟦P65⟧ is designed for the case⟦P66⟧when you're expecting only a single row, omitting the overhead of returning⟦P67⟧an ⟦P68⟧ that includes only one row. You can use the ⟦P69⟧ function⟦P70⟧to make database changes with ⟦P71⟧ statements such as ⟦P72⟧, ⟦P73⟧, or⟦P74⟧⟦P75⟧.⟦P76⟧For more, see the following:⟦P77⟧⟦P78⟧Executing ⟦P79⟧ statements that don't return data⟦PB1_80⟧⟦P81⟧⟦P82⟧Querying for data⟦PB1_83⟧⟦P84⟧Transactions ⟦P85⟧⟦P86⟧Through ⟦P87⟧, you can write code to execute database operations in a⟦P88⟧transaction. In a transaction, multiple operations can be performed together⟦P89⟧and conclude with a final commit, to apply all the changes in one atomic⟦P90⟧step, or a rollback, to discard them.⟦P91⟧For more about transactions, see ⟦P92⟧Executing transactions⟦PB1_93⟧.⟦P94⟧Query cancellation ⟦P95⟧⟦P96⟧You can use ⟦P97⟧ when you want the ability to cancel a database⟦P98⟧operation, such as when the client's connection closes or the operation runs⟦P99⟧longer than you want it to.⟦P100⟧For any database operation, you can use a ⟦P101⟧ package function⟦P102⟧that takes ⟦P103⟧ as an argument. Using the ⟦P104⟧, you can specify a⟦P105⟧timeout or deadline for the operation. You can also use the ⟦P106⟧ to⟦P107⟧propagate a cancellation request through your application to the function⟦P108⟧executing an ⟦P109⟧ statement, ensuring that resources are freed up if they're⟦P110⟧no longer needed.⟦P111⟧For more, see ⟦P112⟧Canceling in-progress operations⟦PB1_113⟧.⟦P114⟧Managed connection pool ⟦P115⟧⟦P116⟧When you use the ⟦P117⟧ database handle, you're connecting with a built-in⟦P118⟧connection pool that creates and disposes of connections according to your⟦P119⟧code's needs. A handle through ⟦P120⟧ is the most common way to do⟦P121⟧database access with Go. For more, see⟦P122⟧⟦P123⟧Opening a database handle⟦PB1_124⟧.⟦P125⟧The ⟦P126⟧ package manages the connection pool for you. However, for⟦P127⟧more advanced needs, you can set connection pool properties as described in⟦P128⟧⟦P129⟧Setting connection pool properties⟦PB1_130⟧.⟦P131⟧For those operations in which you need a single reserved connection, the⟦P132⟧⟦P133⟧ package provides ⟦P134⟧⟦P135⟧⟦PB1_136⟧.⟦P137⟧⟦P138⟧ is especially useful when a transaction with ⟦P139⟧ would be a⟦P140⟧poor choice.⟦P141⟧For example, your code might need to:⟦P142⟧Make schema changes through a ⟦P143⟧, including logic that contains its⟦P144⟧own transaction semantics. Mixing ⟦P145⟧ package transaction functions with⟦P146⟧⟦P147⟧ transaction statements is a poor practice, as described in⟦P148⟧⟦P149⟧Executing transactions⟦PB1_150⟧.⟦P151⟧Perform query locking operations that create temporary tables.⟦P152⟧For more, see ⟦P153⟧Using dedicated connections⟦PB1_154⟧.⟦P155⟧
